\caption(The reviewer retains its best proposal so far. Displacement appends the old incumbent to the FIFO tail; no proposer repeats a reviewer.)
\alttext(Three native boxes show P0 held by R0, P1 displacing P0 at R0, and P0 accepted by R1. The actual free queue after each decision is [1], then [0], then empty.)

> Feasibility first, then blocking pairs

A candidate must first be a one-to-one pairing: both maps must have the correct
length, valid IDs and mutually inverse assignments. A repeated partner or
inconsistent inverse map is not a matching, even when a careless preference
check finds no blocking pair.

Let rank values start at one, with smaller better. A pair(i,j) blocks a feasible
candidate M when both inequalities hold:

$$
\rho_P(i,j)<\rho_P(i,M(i)),\qquad
\rho_R(j,i)<\rho_R(j,M^{-1}(j)).
$$
\alttext(A blocking proposer-reviewer pair strictly prefers each other to their current partners according to both one-based rank lists.)

For a feasible partial candidate, an unmatched incumbent is worse than every
acceptable partner and receives virtual rank n+1 in diagnostics.
Complete equal-side preferences guarantee a perfect outcome: if a free proposer
had exhausted all n reviewers, all reviewers would already be holding a proposer,
leaving no proposer free. Each proposer advances down its list without repeats,
so the finite process terminates. Reviewers only improve their held proposals.
If a proposer preferred some reviewer to its final partner, that reviewer must
have rejected it and ends with someone it prefers; that pair cannot block.

The last previously free reviewer receives just one proposal before all n
reviewers are engaged and the algorithm finishes. Every other reviewer receives
at most n proposals, giving the individual-proposal upper bound

$$
Q(n)=
\begin{cases}
0,&n=0,\\
n(n-1)+1,&n>0.
\end{cases}
$$
\alttext(The sequential proposal bound is zero for an empty market and n times n minus one plus one otherwise.)

This counts sequential FIFO proposals. It is distinct from the simultaneous
stage bound in Gale and Shapley's original presentation.

> Rerun the original five-agent synthetic example

The fixture shuffles strict rankings with mt19937_64 seed 20240927, all proposer
rows before reviewer rows. It is synthetic data, not actual residency records.
Native rank summaries and orientation reversal replace the original report's
local rank search and manual map swap. When the sides exchange roles,
reviewer_match in that execution becomes proposer_match in the original
orientation; the executed proposal count is retained as metadata.

The old report's cached tables showed mean resident ranks 1.2 and 3.2.
Rerunning its unchanged program in this toolchain instead gives 1.8 and 2.8,
with nine resident-proposing proposals. The new core preserves the actual
pre-edit execution. Standard-library shuffle mapping is not specified across
implementations, so a seed alone does not make these exact preference rows
portable. Keep the rows or the toolchain when exact numeric reproduction matters.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/gale_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
