\alttext(Paired blue and red points show resident ranks2 versus3,4 versus4,1 versus1,1 versus3 and1 versus3 for residents0 through4.)

The [NRMP's own description](https://www.nrmp.org/intro-to-the-match/how-matching-algorithm-works/)
uses applicant-proposing tentative matches and program positions.
It also processes [joint program choices for couples](https://www.nrmp.org/residency-applicants/get-ready-for-the-match/couples-in-the-match/).
Those capacities and coupled lists exceed this library's strict complete
one-to-one model. The toy example illustrates a building block, not the complete
NRMP system or a claim about actual applicant outcomes.

> Proposer-optimal means optimal among stable matchings

[Gale and Shapley (1962), College Admissions and the Stability of Marriage](https://doi.org/10.1080/00029890.1962.11989827)
prove existence in Theorem 1, printed pages 12–13, and proposer-side optimality
in Theorem 2, page 14 ([full paper](https://uvammm.github.io/docs/gale-shapley.pdf)).
Their first three-agent example has three stable matchings, which we enumerate
below.

A useful proof invariant is that a rejected pair can never occur in a stable
matching. Consider the first rejection that supposedly destroys such a pair.
The reviewer prefers its held proposer. That held proposer has already been
rejected by every more-preferred reviewer, so by induction none could be its
partner in a stable alternative. An alternative assigning the rejected pair
would therefore be blocked by the held proposer and this reviewer.
Thus every proposer ends with its best attainable stable partner.

The same argument also gives reviewer-pessimality: if a stable alternative gave
some reviewer a worse partner, its current proposer would prefer that reviewer
to its alternative stable partner, forming a blocking pair.
The comparison is within the stable set. It does not say everyone benefits,
or that ordinal mean rank is the unique objective of a matching policy.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/gale_stable_set.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/stable_matching.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <algorithm>
#include <array>
#include <cmath>
#include <limits>
#include <print>
namespace dm=datamunge;
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const dm::algorithms::StablePreferences p{{0,1,2},{1,2,0},{2,0,1}},r{{1,2,0},{2,0,1},{0,1,2}};
  const auto all=dm::algorithms::enumerate_stable_matchings(p,r,6);
  const auto forward=dm::algorithms::gale_shapley(p,r);
  const auto backward=dm::algorithms::reverse_stable_matching(dm::algorithms::gale_shapley(r,p));
  std::print("Paper example: {} perfect candidates tested; {} stable matchings; actual blocking-check pair tests {}.\n",all.candidates_tested,all.matchings.size(),all.pairs_examined);
  std::print("\n| proposer map | proposer ranks | reviewer ranks | mean proposer rank | mean reviewer rank |\n| --- | --- | --- | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::vector<double>pm,rm;std::vector<std::string>labels;
  for(const auto& m:all.matchings){const auto ranks=dm::algorithms::stable_matching_ranks(m,p,r);std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {:.1f} | {:.1f} |\n",m.proposer_match,ranks.proposer_ranks,ranks.reviewer_ranks,ranks.proposer_mean,ranks.reviewer_mean);pm.push_back(ranks.proposer_mean);rm.push_back(ranks.reviewer_mean);labels.push_back(std::format("{}",m.proposer_match));}
  std::print("\nProposer-optimal map {}; reviewer-optimal map {}. Enumerated candidates have proposal metadata 0.\n",forward.proposer_match,backward.proposer_match);
  const auto f=dm::algorithms::stable_matching_example_preferences();
  const auto original=dm::algorithms::enumerate_stable_matchings(f.proposer_preferences,f.reviewer_preferences,120);
  std::print("Original five-agent rows: {} perfect candidates tested; {} stable matchings.\n",original.candidates_tested,original.matchings.size());
  auto left=dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot(pm,labels);left.title("Proposer ranks").xlab("Stable proposer map").ylab("Mean one-based rank; lower is preferred").y_limits(0,3.5);
  auto right=dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot(rm,labels);right.title("Reviewer ranks").xlab("Stable proposer map").ylab("Mean one-based rank; lower is preferred").y_limits(0,3.5);
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2).add(left).add(right).size(1100,520).title("All three stable candidates in the paper's first example").fit_panels().save_svg("build/gale_stable_set.svg");
  if(all.matchings.size()!=3||all.candidates_tested!=6||original.matchings.empty()||original.candidates_tested!=120)throw std::runtime_error("complete stable sets");
}
