\caption(The three stable maps give proposer/reviewer mean ranks(1,3),(2,2),(3,1). The middle candidate is also stable; stability alone does not select a unique distribution of outcomes.)
\alttext(Two native bar panels show proposer means 1,2,3 and reviewer means 3,2,1 across the three stable maps. The opposite proposal orientations select opposite ends.)

Enumeration tests every perfect permutation and uses the same blocking checker.
It is a bounded demonstration, not an independent oracle. The development tests
instead scan preference positions directly and compare the complete stable sets
for every strict profile through three agents. The native enumeration admits
the entire n! search bound before it begins; its cap counts candidates tested,
not just stable answers returned. It can take factorial time and output space.

> Count proposals without overlooking rank preparation

The mutual-first family needs exactly n proposals. If every preference row on
both sides is 0,1,…,n-1, this FIFO schedule uses n(n+1)/2 proposals:
proposer 0 is accepted immediately, proposer 1 after two attempts, and so on.
These are measured counts, not elapsed-time results or a claim that this family
attains every worst-case bound.

Both families still validate n² preference entries per side and write an n²
reviewer inverse-rank table before matching. The ordinary implementation
therefore uses O(n²) time and storage even when the proposal phase is short.
The work counters below exclude validation, allocation and trace copies.
Full snapshots can add O(nQ) time and retained storage, hence cubic worst-case
history, while the ordinary FIFO/maps themselves need only linear state.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/gale_work.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/stable_matching.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <algorithm>
#include <array>
#include <cmath>
#include <limits>
#include <print>
namespace dm=datamunge;
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  std::vector<double>x,best_y,common_y,rank_y;
  std::print("| n | mutual-first proposals | common-order proposals | common immediate rejections | free accepts | reviewer-rank writes | proposal upper bound |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(std::size_t n:std::array<std::size_t,7>{8,16,32,64,128,256,512}){
    const auto first=dm::algorithms::stable_matching_mutual_first_preferences(n);
    const auto common=dm::algorithms::stable_matching_common_preferences(n);
    const auto a=dm::algorithms::gale_shapley_work(first.proposer_preferences,first.reviewer_preferences);
    const auto b=dm::algorithms::gale_shapley_work(common.proposer_preferences,common.reviewer_preferences);
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",n,a.result.proposals,b.result.proposals,b.work.rejections,b.work.accepted_free,b.work.rank_entries,dm::algorithms::gale_shapley_proposal_bound(n));
    x.push_back(std::log2(double(n)));best_y.push_back(std::log2(double(a.result.proposals)));common_y.push_back(std::log2(double(b.result.proposals)));rank_y.push_back(std::log2(double(b.work.rank_entries)));
    if(a.result.proposals!=n||b.result.proposals!=n*(n+1)/2||b.work.rank_entries!=n*n)throw std::runtime_error("native family counts");
  }
  auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  plot.lines(x,best_y,"Best proposals",{37,99,235},2).points(x,best_y,"",{37,99,235},4)
    .lines(x,common_y,"Common proposals",{220,38,38},2).points(x,common_y,"",{220,38,38},4)
    .lines(x,rank_y,"Rank writes",{22,163,74},2)
    .legend({"Best proposals","Common proposals","Rank writes"},{{37,99,235},{220,38,38},{22,163,74}})
    .title("Actual proposal counts and mandatory reviewer-rank writes")
    .xlab("log2 agents on each side").ylab("log2 actual counted operations").y_limits(2,22).size(1100,540).save_svg("build/gale_work.svg");
}
